1
00:01:03,296 --> 00:01:11,609
Good morning. Today I am going to cover the daunting task of giving a quality speech - a thought that makes most of us cringe. 

2
00:01:12,164 --> 00:01:20,714
In fact, 90% of all people feel nervous about public speaking, about 10% of whom are described as genuinely terrified. 

3
00:01:20,714 --> 00:01:28,421
Hopefully when we are finished here today, you all will be in the 10% of the population who do not feel nervous at all.


4
00:01:28,421 --> 00:01:35,531
Did you know that lecturers tend to get more nervous if the speech they are giving is an important one? It makes sense, right? 

5
00:01:35,531 --> 00:01:48,047
You probably wouldn’t be nearly as nervous to address your residence hall about the proper use of the recycle and compost bins as you might be if you were asked to give the graduation speech to your entire 5000-student class.


6
00:01:48,047 --> 00:01:58,767
So what is it that makes some people completely comfortable in front of crowds? Some people think that the ability to give a good speech is a gift that others are simply born with. 

7
00:01:58,767 --> 00:02:03,580
This is almost never the case. Public speaking can be learned with practice.


8
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:11,452
The first, most important thing you can do to improve your confidence in delivering a speech is to prepare a quality speech. 

9
00:02:11,452 --> 00:02:20,146
Honestly, while the content of your speech is relatively important, the audience will really only remember the last sentence you say.


10
00:02:20,146 --> 00:02:27,083
It is a good idea to structure the rest of your speech to lead up to this last point to really drive your message home. 

11
00:02:27,453 --> 00:02:30,710
This is a good way to ensure that your speech is well-organized.


12
00:02:35,941 --> 00:02:41,984
Once you are confident in the quality of the speech you have written, the rest is just about your stage presence. 

13
00:02:41,984 --> 00:02:45,497
Let’s go through some do’s and don’ts of public speaking.


14
00:02:45,497 --> 00:02:55,005
First, you want to command the attention of the room. Do not - I repeat, do not proceed with your speech until the audience is paying attention. 

15
00:02:55,005 --> 00:03:03,739
Even Martin Luther King, Junior’s I Have a Dream speech wouldn’t have made any difference in the world without the undivided attention of his audience.


16
00:03:03,739 --> 00:03:14,450
To continue with the I Have a Dream example, one of the reasons that MLK was such an effective orator was his ability to speak with passion and engage with his audience. 

17
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:18,526
That sort of charisma does not come from reading straight from paper. 

18
00:03:18,526 --> 00:03:25,576
Don’t get me wrong - it is a good idea to write your main ideas down on a note card, sheet of paper, or something. 

19
00:03:25,576 --> 00:03:32,803
But one factor that will consistently lead to a boring, forgettable speech is writing down your entire speech. 

20
00:03:32,803 --> 00:03:35,599
Do not write your full speech down. 

21
00:03:35,599 --> 00:03:45,231
If you are constantly reading your paper, you are not making eye contact with your audience, and thus failing to really express the feeling that goes with your ideas.


22
00:03:45,231 --> 00:03:53,420
I advise you to write one or two ideas, so if you suddenly draw a blank you have something to jog your memory.

23
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:59,213
 If you’ve written a good speech that you believe in, those ideas should be sufficient to keep you on track.


24
00:03:59,213 --> 00:04:11,857
Once you have those ideas written down, give your speech a few practice runs in front of the mirror, into your sound recorder on your phone, or with a friend before it comes time to address a crowd. 


25
00:04:11,857 --> 00:04:21,620
That way you can hear how the ideas come across, make sure there are no abrupt transitions, and find out whether you’re talking too fast or too slow. 

26
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:27,776
Timing is important - make sure you time yourself beforehand to see how long your speech is.


27
00:04:27,776 --> 00:04:35,776
That is pretty much it! With practice you’ll be able to deliver an expert speech that engages and even maybe inspires your audience. 

28
00:04:35,776 --> 00:04:41,450
Just remember - speak with emotion. No one wants to listen to someone reading from a script.


29
00:04:41,450 --> 00:04:48,614
As I come to a close in my speech, I’ll point out that I have employed all of these tips that I have covered. 

30
00:04:48,614 --> 00:04:59,623
I practiced my speech ahead of time and timed it, and I can even show you my one small index card with just three simple bullets on it. It’s as easy as that!